results/
python_models/results/

# nebula experiment output (regenerate: python -m nebula.experiments.<name>)
#
# G49, AS AMENDED 2026-08-06: "regenerable" is not the test. An experiment's
# output is TRACKED if any deliverable quotes a number from it — a results file
# that took longer to produce than it takes to review is an INPUT to the
# write-up, not a build artifact. `s9_yield_results.json` was in this list, and
# when session 11 wanted the per-design coordinates behind S9_YIELD.md §4 it
# cost 7560 fresh SPICE runs / 23 min to get back. It has been REMOVED from
# this list and is now tracked.
#
# Session 13 adds three more files that are TRACKED under the same rule, and
# they are listed here rather than left to silence so the choice is visible:
#   nebula/experiments/s9_yield_tail_results.json  (the re-run with a real tail)
#   nebula/experiments/tail_device_data.csv        (261 rows, TAIL_DEVICE.md 3-6)
#   nebula/experiments/tail_device_results.json
# Note also that `s9_yield.py`'s DEFAULT output name now depends on whether the
# tail is ideal, so session 12b's file cannot be overwritten by a later run with
# a different circuit in it. It already was once, during session 13's plumbing
# checks, and was only recoverable because it was tracked.
#
# Session 17 (task 6) adds three more, TRACKED, and the reason is stronger than
# G49's: these are not only inputs to `nebula/RL_SMOKE.md`, they are FREE
# TRAINING DATA. Every row is a real SKY130 evaluation with a `design_id`, which
# is exactly what task 8's surrogate needs for a grouped train/test split. The
# 500-step run cost 26.5 minutes of ngspice; regenerating it to recover rows
# that were thrown away would cost that twice.
#   nebula/experiments/rl_smoke_results.json           (gates, calibration, runs)
#   nebula/experiments/rl_smoke_run_v0.jsonl           (765 evaluations, v0)
#   nebula/experiments/rl_smoke_run_v1.jsonl           (319 evaluations, v1)
#   nebula/experiments/rl_smoke_parallel_reversed.json (G71: the SAME sweep with
#       the worker counts reversed. Kept as a SEPARATE file rather than folded
#       into the results, because the pair is the evidence -- one of them is a
#       cold-cache artifact and neither is interpretable alone.)
#
# The two still ignored here are ignored DELIBERATELY and at a known cost: the
# copies on disk are stale relative to the numbers BOUNDS_REDERIVATION.md and
# CL_SENSITIVITY.md publish, so tracking them would ship a file that disagrees
# with its own write-up. Their published figures are re-derivable only by
# re-running (~3 min per row, seeded).
s3_yield_results.json
cl_sensitivity_results.json
analytic_screen_results.json

# Run locks (experiments/runlock.py). Ephemeral machine-local state: a pid and a
# start time, meaningful only on the box that wrote it. `runlock.py` and its
# tests ARE tracked; the lock files it drops are not. A committed lock would also
# arrive on another checkout looking like a live run and refuse to start one.
*.runlock.json

# ngspice scratch. run_point() works in a TemporaryDirectory, but interactive
# sessions in nebula/device/spice/ leave these behind.
*.raw
*.log
swing.txt
nebula/device/spice/*.csv
nebula/device/spice/*.txt
nebula/device/spice/*.out

# ams_rl_ppo reference implementation (CLAUDEwa §4.2): its SOURCE is tracked,
# its shipped checkpoints and training logs are not. They are the artifacts of
# the degenerate result table §4.2 says explicitly not to anchor on.
*.pt
*.npz
policy*.zip
train_summary*.json

# ...but OUR OWN measured tables are results, and G49 is the standing reminder
# that a gitignored result costs the time it took to make. The rule above
# exists to exclude ams_rl_ppo's SHIPPED CHECKPOINTS, not our measurements, so
# the un-ignore is explicit and narrow -- same treatment as our trimmed SKY130
# library above.
#
# gmid_lut_*.npz is 27.6 MB and IS regenerable, deterministically, by
#   python -m nebula.device.gmid_lut --build --workers 6      (510 s measured)
# It is tracked anyway because GMID_MAP.md's numbers are derived through it and
# because its provenance header pins the library SHA that produced it. If the
# size ever becomes the binding problem, the regeneration command above is the
# thing to fall back on -- but make that decision deliberately, in the handoff,
# rather than by adding an ignore rule.
!nebula/device/data/gmid_lut_*.npz

